 Useful if you want to… federate several IdPs without running Keycloak or another external IAM;
keep one OIDC issuer and one login surface for Gate One;
normalize IdP-specific groups, roles and claims into one vocabulary;
assign roles/scopes/claims to local and federated users;
stay lightweight: one Go binary, one JSON file, Redis optional for multi-instance state.
cladone-auth is optional. It is a small OIDC issuer and user directory, not a full identity provider.
What it does federates external IdPs configured under tenants[].upstreams;
lets an admin map upstream IAM fields into a common roles/scopes/claims model;
stores one external identity link (issuer, subject) per federated user — the IdP account is never recreated;
stores the same roles/scopes/claims grants on local users;
provisions federated users at first login (JIT) without rights, then lets an admin assign them;
emits Ed25519-signed ID/access/refresh tokens, JWKS, discovery and the revocation keyspace Gate One reads.
The embedded IAM is deliberately resource-light. The recommended path uses external IdPs, so there is no password store and no password-hashing cost. Local accounts (localAccounts, PBKDF2) are optional for small deployments.
Mapping IdP claims to application rights A mapping rule takes a claim path and adds IAM roles, scopes or claims:
IdP claim common rights groups=admins -> role admin realm_access.roles=offline... -> role user department=support -> scope read:things, claim department=support Effective rights are:
tenant defaults + admin emails + mapped IdP claims (federated only) + per-user grants (local and federated) Mapping results are recalculated at every federated login. Per-user grants are stored on the user and apply to both kinds of account.
Identity model A user has exactly one authentication identity:
local: email + password managed by the IAM;
federated: one (issuer, subject) link.
There is no multi-link/account-linking model. If an upstream subject changes unexpectedly, an admin relinks the existing IAM user instead of losing its grants.
Administration cladone-auth user provides list, show, create, set and delete for local and federated users. The same commands assign roles, scopes and claims, disable an account or relink a federated identity.
Grant changes take effect at the next login; revoke sessions for immediate invalidation.
Example and reference Embedded IAM example — IAM configuration, IdP mapping, user creation and grants.
Embedded IAM configuration reference
tenants[.mappings]
Register every application origin in tenants[.postLogoutRedirectUris] so a logout can return to the app; leaving it empty falls back to redirectUris (the login host only) and the logout is refused — see Logout and revocation.
Important Not a full IAM suite: no admin UI, no multi-identity linking, no per-resource policy engine.
Route policy stays in Gate One.
Authentication adapters and IAM IdP adapters remain under the Gate One licence.
If you already run Keycloak or Zitadel, use it as the external IAM instead: Multiple identity providers.

 A single Gate One instance keeps its OIDC session and revocation state in a local bbolt file. That is enough for one instance and needs no Redis.
bbolt is local to the instance and cannot be shared safely by several Gate One replicas: a logout or a back-channel revocation recorded on one node would not be visible to the others. As soon as a second replica runs, that shared state moves to Redis.
What a scaled deployment requires Redis as the shared session/revocation store (authProvider.sessionStore.type: "redis");
the same session signing key on every node (authProvider.sessionKeyEnv);
the same cookie domain, so the browser presents the same session cookie to every replica;
a load balancer in front of the replicas (Reverse proxy);
Redis Sentinel/TLS/ACL when Redis itself must be highly available.
No sticky sessions are required for the session cookie: every replica verifies it with the shared key. Redis carries the shared revocation state.
Failure mode When sessionStore is Redis and Redis is unavailable, authenticated routes fail with 502 authentication_unavailable. Public routes keep working. A single-instance deployment should keep the default bbolt store — Redis is a scaling requirement, not a default.
Related Horizontal scaling — when and why.
High Availability example — the Redis-backed configuration and the shared session key.
authProvider.sessionStore and [sessionKeyEnv](/docs/config/gate-one/#field-authProvider-sessionKeyEnv).

 Useful if you want to… run more than one Gate One instance behind a load balancer;
survive the loss of one replica without losing the login;
spread load across several replicas;
keep logout and revocation effective across every replica.
Why a single instance is simpler A single Gate One instance keeps its OIDC session/revocation state in a local bbolt file. That is enough for one instance, and it needs no Redis.
bbolt is local to the instance. It cannot be shared safely by several Gate One replicas: a logout or back-channel revocation recorded on one node would not be visible to the others. That is why horizontal scaling requires a shared store.
What horizontal scaling requires Redis as the shared session/revocation store (authProvider.sessionStore.type: "redis");
the same session signing key on every node (authProvider.sessionKeyEnv);
the same cookie domain, so the browser presents the same session cookie to every replica;
a load balancer in front of the replicas;
Redis Sentinel/TLS/ACL when Redis itself must be highly available.
No sticky sessions are required for the session cookie itself: every replica can verify it with the shared key. Redis carries the shared revocation state.
Failure mode When sessionStore is Redis and Redis is unavailable, authenticated routes fail with 502 authentication_unavailable. Public routes keep working.
A single-instance deployment should keep the default bbolt store. Redis is a scaling requirement, not a default.
