 Two examples with Cloudflare’s test keys: a form (mode: forms) and a whole-page gate (mode: gate). The test keys pass without a real widget; replace them before deploying.
Form protection [examples/turnstile/routes.json](/examples/turnstile/routes.json)
{ "turnstile": { "siteKey": "1x00000000000000000000AA", "secretEnv": "TURNSTILE_SECRET", "paths": ["/contact/*"], "forms": ["form#contact"] }, "authProvider": { "type": "dev", "loginBaseUrl": "http://login.demo.localhost:3000", "cookieDomain": "demo.localhost" }, "routes": [{ "host": "app.demo.localhost", "path": "/*", "frontend": true, "backend": "http://127.0.0.1:8080" }] } Whole-page gate [examples/turnstile-gate/routes.json](/examples/turnstile-gate/routes.json)
{ "turnstile": { "mode": "gate", "siteKey": "1x00000000000000000000AA", "secretEnv": "TURNSTILE_SECRET", "paths": ["/*"], "exemptPaths": ["/healthz"], "clearanceTtlSeconds": 1800, "challenge": { "appearance": "interaction-only" } }, "routes": [{ "host": "app.demo.localhost", "path": "/*", "frontend": true, "backend": "http://127.0.0.1:8080" }] } Run it export TURNSTILE_SECRET=1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA gate-one -check -config routes.json gate-one -config routes.json Open http://app.demo.localhost:3000/.
forms: the widget is injected into form#contact; a POST without a token is refused by Gate One.
gate: the first visit shows "Checking your browser"; after the test challenge, the page is served and the clearance cookie lasts 30 minutes.
The source repository also ships a runnable demo (deploy/local/turnstile) with a backend, ./run.sh gate and ./run.sh api.
Production checklist create the widget in the Cloudflare dashboard and declare every public hostname;
put the secret in the environment of every replica (TURNSTILE_SECRET), never in the file;
keep a rateLimit on the protected paths;
restrict the application’s network access to Gate One: this check only covers traffic that goes through it;
document that the client IP is sent to Cloudflare;
decide whether the site must stay indexable (a /* gate blocks crawlers).
Limits the token is single-use: only Gate One verifies it;
a second ingress that reaches the application directly bypasses the check;
a gate on /* blocks search engines.
